6. Scope and Namespaces
This is one of the most important concepts today.
Scope determines where a variable can be accessed.
Consider:
x = 100

def example():
    x = 50
    print(x)

example()
print(x)
output:
50
100
Why?
There are two different variables named x:
•	Global x → 100
•	Local x → 50
The local variable doesn't overwrite the global variable.
Local scope
Variables created inside a function are generally local to that function.
def calculate():
    result = 100
    print(result)

calculate()
Trying to access result outside the function causes a NameError.
Global scope
Variables defined at the module level can generally be accessed throughout that module.
name = "Pramod"

def greet():
    print(name)

greet()
output:
Pramod
The global keyword
What if you actually want to modify a global variable?
count = 10

def update():
    global count
    count = 20

update()

print(count)
output:
20
global tells Python that you're referring to the module-level variable.
Development advice: Avoid using global variables unnecessarily. Passing values through function parameters and returning results usually produces more maintainable code.
